<div class="space-y-6">
  <section class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5">
    <h2 class="mb-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-stone-500">Authorisation</h2>
    <dl class="space-y-2 text-sm">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
        <dt class="w-40 text-stone-500">Status</dt>
        <dd><%= herd_status_badge(instance) %></dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5">
    <h2 class="mb-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-stone-500">Cross-instance signals</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-700">
      <strong><%= instance.participating? ? "On" : "Off" %></strong> —
      <% if instance.participating? %>
        this herd takes part, so your applicants are matched against theirs and
        theirs against yours: an address or network already in use on the other
        side shows as a flag. Only the domain is ever exchanged.
      <% else %>
        this herd does not take part, so nothing is matched in either direction.
      <% end %>
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-stone-500">
      Taking part needs two keys — the instance admin's <code>signals=on</code>
      in the DNS record, and this waterhole's operator approving it — and it is
      reciprocal on purpose: a herd only sees the signal if it contributes to it.
      <%= link_to "Your own settings", herd_path(current_instance), class: "underline" %>
      show which of the two keys yours has turned.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
